@@@@ open-binary-search-invariants
situation: open
topic: Binary search and its invariants
voice: plain
level: medium
expect: {"must":["invariant"],"askMustNot":["lo = mid`? (is|loops|never)","never shrinks","stays the same","two elements"]}
noverify: an opening; it asks which update can loop for ever and states no result
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

Binary search finds a position in sorted data in about $\log_2 n$ steps. It is the lookup inside a B-tree page, in the sorted tables of an LSM store, in `git bisect` and in `std::lower_bound`. The idea fits in one sentence, but the code is known for off-by-one bugs: a loop that never ends, or a target at the edge that is missed. An invariant, a statement about `lo` and `hi` that stays true on every pass, is what lets you check each line instead of guessing it. The same reasoning carries over to finding a boundary and to binary search on an answer.

<ask type="predict">A binary search keeps `lo` and `hi` as inclusive bounds, loops `while lo <= hi` and takes `mid = (lo + hi) // 2`. When `a[mid]` is smaller than the target, one version sets `lo = mid` and another sets `lo = mid + 1`. Which version can loop for ever, and on what size of range does it get stuck?</ask>

