noverify: an opening; the question's answer (the leftover vertices contain a cycle) is not stated
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

A topological sort puts the vertices of a directed graph in an order where every edge points forwards. Build systems such as Make and Bazel use it to order compilation, package managers to order installs, and schedulers such as Airflow to order the tasks of a pipeline. You have the graph model and Kahn's algorithm from two days ago; what the algorithm does on a graph that has no valid order was the one gap, and it is what those tools rely on to reject a bad dependency file.

<ask type="short">Kahn's algorithm runs on a graph with 6 vertices. Its queue becomes empty after it has output only 4 of them. What does that tell you about the 2 vertices left over, and why did neither ever enter the queue?</ask>

@@@@ open-prior-kruskal-on-union-find
situation: open-prior
topic: Kruskal's algorithm with union-find
voice: plain
level: medium
before: {"prior":[{"topic":"Union-find with path compression","daysAgo":4,"finished":true,"plan":["Sets as trees with a parent array","Find and union","Path compression","Union by rank"],"right":5,"partly":1,"wrong":0,"missed":[]}]}
expect: {"must":["union-find","cheapest"],"askMustNot":["find\\(","\\broots?\\b","same set"]}
noverify: an opening; it asks how the cycle test is done and does not state it
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

A minimum spanning tree connects every vertex of a weighted graph for the lowest total edge weight. It is used to lay out networks, in single-linkage clustering, and as a step inside approximation algorithms. Kruskal's algorithm builds one by taking the edges from cheapest to dearest and skipping any edge that would close a cycle. That test rests on the union-find structure from your lesson four days ago: each tree of the forest built so far is one set. With it the whole algorithm costs $O(E \log E)$, and the sort of the edges is the dominant term.

<ask type="short">Kruskal's algorithm is about to consider the edge between vertices u and v. With the two union-find operations you already have, how does it decide whether adding this edge would close a cycle?</ask>

